  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 AM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nTODAY, No major changes from the previous forecast. Snow was \nfalling across southwest Minnesota as expected, with MNDOT \nshowing some partially to mostly covered roads on the 511 page. \nBeing the first snow of the season, we did issue a special \nweather statement to raise awareness for potential slick spots \novernight into early Wednesday morning. Road conditions should \nquickly improve throughout the day as afternoon highs climb into\nthe upper 30s.\n\nTHURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY, Expect seasonable temperatures along\nwith mostly dry weather, leading to favorable travel \nconditions. The weak system that brought this morning's snow\nwill move southeast and develop into an impressive mid-latitude\ncyclone. Closer to home, high pressure will build over the\nUpper Midwest keeping winds light and conditions dry for\nThanksgiving. \n\nOn Friday, an upper level wave will move across the\nInternational Border and drag a weak cold front through the\nregion. This could bring small chances (10 to 20 percent) of\nlight rain or snow showers, but little if any accumulation is\nexpected.\n\nLooking ahead, cooler air will follow for Sunday as high\npressure builds across the region. On Monday and Tuesday, there\nis a signal for a larger storm system to approach from the west\nbrining southerly flow, increased moisture, and small chances\n(10 to 15 percent) for rain (with maybe some snow mixed in). But\nthere is considerable disagreement in the timing, placement, and\nstrength of this system, so continued with the blended guidance.\nWe will continue to monitor this one, but overall December looks\nto pick up where November left off, meaning dry and mild.",
